 ## Overview
-Hybrid Implicit Isosurface Ray Tracing is a rendering technique that combines explicit voxel selection with implicit ray-surface intersection to render high-quality, crack-free isosurfaces from rectilinear or adaptive mesh refinement (AMR) volumes [S1, unofficial][S2]. Unlike sample-based methods that use fixed step sizes and often miss surface details in refined regions, this method builds a Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH) over active voxel primitives and performs exact implicit intersections within each primitive, avoiding the memory overhead of explicit polygon extraction [S1, unofficial][S2]. It is particularly suited for interactive visualization of large-scale AMR data where surface continuity and detail preservation are critical [S1, unofficial].
+Hybrid Implicit Isosurface Ray Tracing is a rendering technique that extracts a list of active vertex-centered cubic primitives (octants or dual cells) from cell-centered TB-AMR data, builds a BVH over them, and performs implicit ray-isosurface intersections within each primitive, avoiding the memory cost of explicit triangle extraction [S2]. It combines explicit extraction (selecting active voxels) with implicit tracing (intersecting rays against the trilinear field within those voxels) to produce high-quality, crack-free isosurfaces [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sample-based isosurface methods that use fixed step sizes and may miss features in refined regions, this method adapts to the local resolution, providing more detail and better framerates for AMR data [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates by treating the volume as a source of logical voxel primitives (octants or dual cells), filtering those that intersect the target isovalue, and then ray tracing through a BVH of these primitives to find intersections implicitly.
+The method operates on rectilinear volumes (regular grids, rectilinear grids, BS-AMR) by treating them as a source of logical voxels. Each voxel is a cube with trilinearly interpolated scalar values at its vertices [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input:** A rectilinear volume or Tree-Based AMR (TB-AMR) dataset. The data is typically cell-centered.
-*   **Output:** A rendered image of the isosurface defined by a user-specified isovalue $\tau$.
+*   **Input:** A rectilinear volume (e.g., TB-AMR data) and a user-specified isovalue.
+*   **Output:** A rendered image of the isosurface.
 
