 ### Parameters and Edge Cases
-*   **Isovalue ($\tau$):** The threshold for surface extraction. Primitives are filtered based on whether $\tau$ lies within the min/max range of their vertex values [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Ray Packet Size:** The intersection routine processes rays in packets of 4, 8, or 16 [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Precision:** The method avoids discretization artifacts inherent in sample-based methods by performing implicit intersections [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary Conditions:** Dual cells are only used in non-boundary regions where vertices are at the same AMR level to prevent overlap with octants [S2].
+*   **Isosvalue:** User-specified scalar value defining the surface [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Ray Packet Size:** 4, 8, or 16 rays [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** The method relies on trilinear interpolation. Degenerate cases where the isovalue equals the min/max vertex value may require careful handling to avoid missed hits or double hits, though the source notes that the method yields a "crack-free" surface [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** The method handles AMR boundaries by using dual cells in non-boundary regions and octants elsewhere, ensuring consistency [S2].
 
